- 2026-11-04: Step 3c built: runners come and go. The rig's runner processes are a rig setting, `runners` (`[{name, pools}]`, at most 16, each named once), which control sends in the signed `rig_settings` command (REST `POST /v1/rigs/<rig>/settings` and MCP `set_rig_settings`, the owner's token only); `THERMITE_RUNNERS` is its value until the owner sets one. The core's RunnerHub (harness/controller/runners.ts) takes each change while it works: a runner process added joins its pools, with a judge's executor for each runner of the judge's or the shared pool (its loop starts then), and takes runs once it answers its check (`info`, every 15 s, 20 s to answer) with the core's kind of target and enough runners; one left out, or given other pools, leaves: its run under way fails at once (RunnerLink.fail, a RunnerAway), the judge queues its job again with no verdict, its calibrations go, its executor's loop ends, and a lane is told to run it again; one that fails two checks in a row is away, its run under way fails the same way, and it takes runs again once it answers, sent what it lacks afresh. A run waits while no runner of its pool is up and free; a wait for a pool no runner serves now ends with that said, and the lane's next run is on the core. The judge's card count follows its runners. At the start every executor's lease is taken back, those of runners gone meanwhile too. The core's supervisor carries the asks always. Pages: Settings, REST API, MCP tools, Signed commands, Limits and pacing. Tests: tests/harness/controller/runners.test.ts, pools.test.ts, runner.test.ts (a measurement whose runner goes away), lease.test.ts, rig-settings-store.test.ts, control's settings test. Next, step 4: control, mockup first.
